Uh, and uh, the purpose of today
is, uh, really to illustrate, uh,

00:04:22.286 --> 00:04:27.682
what happens after, um, uh, uh,
this, this accessibility page.

00:04:27.785 --> 00:04:31.854
The then what, um, following
an accessibility page.

00:04:32.168 --> 00:04:35.550
Uh, I'm Adam Unger, the
co-founder of Accessibilities.

00:04:36.797 --> 00:04:37.360
Adam: Hi, everyone.

00:04:37.361 --> 00:04:39.017
I'm Julian De Los Prados.

00:04:39.061 --> 00:04:42.381
I am the co-founder and head of
technology at Accessibilities.

00:04:43.728 --> 00:04:47.899
Julian : Uh, and  Accessibilities
focuses on, uh, three areas:

00:04:47.915 --> 00:04:50.322
digital, physical, and policy.

00:04:50.599 --> 00:04:54.347
Um, and today we're gonna be speaking
to you about, uh, the digital

00:04:54.348 --> 00:05:00.707
accessibility policies that we see
across many websites that you, you

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:02.282
know, you might see them as well.

00:05:02.509 --> 00:05:08.170
Um, and really what, what we've seen
and, and what we've experienced managing

00:05:08.181 --> 00:05:13.620
the process for our clients, um, is
what we're gonna be talking about today,

00:05:13.762 --> 00:05:18.582
what we've seen and, and, um, how we
advise our clients on how to approach

00:05:18.582 --> 00:05:21.409
these accessibility policy pages.

00:05:21.777 --> 00:05:28.652
Um, an accessibility policy is,
is not covered by a WCAG rule.

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:34.337
There's no WCAG guideline that says
this is a must-have for all websites.

00:05:34.628 --> 00:05:38.913
Um, there's, there's no, um,
uh, uh guideline that says this,

00:05:39.181 --> 00:05:41.433
um, n- is needed specifically.

00:05:41.682 --> 00:05:48.560
Um, however, what we know is that
a person who might rely upon an

00:05:48.561 --> 00:05:53.637
accessibility accommodation or might
have a grievance, um, uh, relies

00:05:53.638 --> 00:05:58.555
on the ability to get in touch with
someone, uh, a company, an organization,

00:05:58.558 --> 00:06:00.889
a business, uh, to plan ahead.

00:06:01.168 --> 00:06:05.916
Um, what we know in, in, in chatting with
this community is that planning ahead

00:06:05.917 --> 00:06:09.673
is, is the key to having a great day.

00:06:09.673 --> 00:06:15.040
And for often, um, you know, many of the
individuals in this community, uh, being

00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:19.178
able to go to your favorite restaurant
or go to your favorite shop, uh,

00:06:19.222 --> 00:06:25.183
understanding what accommodations are in
place often starts with this policy page.

00:06:25.195 --> 00:06:28.522
So, um, planning ahead is, is really key.

00:06:28.798 --> 00:06:34.600
Um, you know, there, there, um, there's
an element to having this policy that

00:06:34.605 --> 00:06:39.342
signals a commitment, uh, on behalf of
your organization that's really important.

00:06:39.342 --> 00:06:43.486
It shows your users and your customers
that, um, you care, and it tells them

00:06:43.486 --> 00:06:46.046
how that they, um, can, can reach you.

00:06:46.301 --> 00:06:51.780
Um, there is some EU directive
and, um, there is some legislation

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:56.635
around Title II entities, uh,
requiring a grievance process.

00:06:56.868 --> 00:07:01.238
Um, but for many of the organizations
that we work with, um, I'm often asked,

00:07:01.238 --> 00:07:05.866
"Is this required?" And, and the honest
answer for of- for many is, is no.

00:07:06.177 --> 00:07:09.222
Um, but it's a, it's
more than a nice-to-have.

00:07:09.227 --> 00:07:11.447
It's, it's a, a great to have.

00:07:15.043 --> 00:07:15.589
Adam: Thanks, Adam.

00:07:16.092 --> 00:07:18.296
Little bit abo- more about this survey.

00:07:18.562 --> 00:07:22.528
It was done with a combination
of automated code analysis and

00:07:22.531 --> 00:07:26.349
human review, and the human
review part is how we interpret

00:07:26.406 --> 00:07:28.397
the human impact on the end user.

00:07:29.303 --> 00:07:33.320
We sourced thousands of public
accessibility policy pages during

00:07:33.322 --> 00:07:37.337
our work managing accessibility
requests and grievance processes

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:40.634
for our clients, and we've done
that for almost seven years now.

00:07:41.519 --> 00:07:45.108
The type of things we look
for are: Does the page load?

00:07:45.493 --> 00:07:46.622
What's it built on?

00:07:46.772 --> 00:07:48.786
And are there people supporting it?

00:07:49.243 --> 00:07:51.531
As well as what happens
after you reach out.

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:56.206
And finally, for this study, there
were no third-party directories or

00:07:56.207 --> 00:07:58.454
lists used in collecting the data.

00:07:58.789 --> 00:08:00.603
This was all sourced by us.

00:08:02.296 --> 00:08:03.621
Next slide please, Adam.

00:08:03.624 --> 00:08:04.094
Thank you.

00:08:04.783 --> 00:08:09.337
So next we dove… Next, before we
dive deeper into the numbers, here's a

00:08:09.337 --> 00:08:11.185
little bit about how we labeled them.

00:08:11.547 --> 00:08:15.983
So first we looked for and ruled out
any site that was parked or no longer

00:08:16.027 --> 00:08:20.142
active to make sure our numbers are
counted against live websites only.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:23.975
Once those are removed, we
looked for active sites that had

00:08:24.238 --> 00:08:28.791
inactive accessibility pages, and
we labeled those pages as dead.

00:08:29.827 --> 00:08:34.750
If our crawler was blocked due to
a timeout or a 403 error or an SSL

00:08:34.751 --> 00:08:40.404
error, those pages went into their
own bucket because while a crawler

00:08:40.405 --> 00:08:43.847
might not be able to reach it, a human
still might be able to get through.

00:08:45.107 --> 00:08:51.727
We then looked for which of the websites
were, uh, co- were built on WordPress

00:08:52.039 --> 00:08:56.834
and which of these used overlays, um,
to solve part of their accessibility.

00:08:58.042 --> 00:09:02.602
So every judgment call we made,
we did it by the numbers, and the

00:09:02.615 --> 00:09:05.838
WordPress numbers might actually be
a little bit undercounted, so keep

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.124
that in mind once we get into that.

00:09:12.103 --> 00:09:18.697
So before we judge exactly what a policy
says, let's talk a little bit about

00:09:18.738 --> 00:09:22.025
how the page works or doesn't work.

00:09:23.317 --> 00:09:30.270
So of the 5,237 accessibility policy
pages that we reviewed, we found

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:33.207
that 60% of them were actually live.

00:09:34.206 --> 00:09:36.621
17% were blocked by our tools.

00:09:36.712 --> 00:09:37.862
We set those aside.

00:09:38.392 --> 00:09:41.556
23% were dead on an
otherwise working site.

00:09:42.302 --> 00:09:48.346
So nearly one in four people who
go looking for help on these sites

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:53.180
for accessibility in our sample are
landing on a dead accessibility page.

00:09:54.762 --> 00:09:56.210
So just think about that.

00:09:56.225 --> 00:10:00.681
The people who are most likely clicking
that link are your website users

00:10:00.681 --> 00:10:03.061
who have accessibility requirements.

00:10:03.604 --> 00:10:08.024
They use their keyboard and likely a
screen reader to navigate to the footer.

00:10:08.532 --> 00:10:12.729
They found a link that says accessibility,
but it didn't lead them anywhere,

00:10:13.011 --> 00:10:18.832
and they had no option to make an
accessibility request or, um, a grievance.

00:10:20.359 --> 00:10:25.174
And remember that 23% excludes any parked
domains and blocked pages, so we believe

00:10:25.174 --> 00:10:27.445
that number may actually be a bit higher.

00:10:30.622 --> 00:10:35.307
So what does a dead accessibility page
look like, and how does it get there?

00:10:36.016 --> 00:10:38.473
Uh, four examples here.

00:10:38.473 --> 00:10:41.455
These are examples that we've
seen in our day-to-day work.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:47.496
First is that a page was moved and the
footer link was simply not updated.

00:10:48.425 --> 00:10:51.753
Uh, we also see these
broken by routine updates.

00:10:51.771 --> 00:10:58.633
Maybe a theme or a plug-in update, uh,
happens and something on the page breaks,

00:10:58.634 --> 00:11:00.979
leading to the link not being active.

00:11:01.492 --> 00:11:04.214
I'm sure many people in this
room are familiar with that.

00:11:05.612 --> 00:11:07.359
We also see it happen during cleanup.

00:11:07.498 --> 00:11:13.749
So a proactive client will go into
their website and clean up and remove

00:11:13.751 --> 00:11:19.521
pages that don't seem to be, uh, current
or trafficked, et cetera, and the

00:11:19.521 --> 00:11:21.716
accessibility page becomes a casualty.

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:23.819
And then finally, in a rebrand.

00:11:23.844 --> 00:11:28.863
So anytime a new site is created, it's
so important that the accessibility

00:11:28.866 --> 00:11:32.984
page is migrated over, and that
leads to a talk about some good

00:11:32.994 --> 00:11:35.396
documentation that we'll touch on later.

00:11:36.238 --> 00:11:40.521
So four stories here, but the
one pattern is, uh, the page

00:11:40.523 --> 00:11:42.345
went down and nobody noticed.

00:11:42.812 --> 00:11:46.636
So let's talk about the root
cause of why these links break.

00:11:47.444 --> 00:11:52.658
We talk about the fact that there's a lack
of owner or an accessibility coordinator

00:11:52.669 --> 00:11:55.202
for many businesses and organizations.

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:59.334
Another common thing we see is
that there's no documentation and

00:11:59.335 --> 00:12:01.244
the page wasn't made a priority.

00:12:02.225 --> 00:12:05.367
That is especially important
when that migration happens.

00:12:05.421 --> 00:12:09.127
Nobody knows the accessibility
page is there and is a priority.

00:12:09.471 --> 00:12:13.029
It often gets left out by a website
development thing, for example.

00:12:14.249 --> 00:12:15.466
Uh, no testing.

00:12:15.874 --> 00:12:20.053
So a check of a website's accessibility
policy is a very common practice for

00:12:20.054 --> 00:12:22.682
most digital accessibility inspectors.

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:28.449
Finally, no monitoring of the site
or of the inbox that receives the

00:12:28.449 --> 00:12:30.881
accessibility requests on the page.

00:12:31.512 --> 00:12:35.544
So simply by having someone in charge
of that inbox, it becomes much easier

00:12:35.544 --> 00:12:37.785
to see when the link becomes inactive.

00:12:41.892 --> 00:12:43.070
And I will

00:12:45.685 --> 00:12:49.943
give it to Adam to talk a
bit about the, uh, page load.

00:12:51.161 --> 00:12:51.531
Julian : Yeah.

00:12:51.531 --> 00:12:59.405
So, um, this is to the point where
a user lands on the page and, uh, it

00:12:59.489 --> 00:13:04.391
loads, and a person might, might get
there, but, uh, what, what happens

00:13:04.395 --> 00:13:09.948
when, uh, that user, customer, a person
in, in this community who might need

00:13:09.948 --> 00:13:13.074
that page, uh, uh, lands on that page?

00:13:13.077 --> 00:13:18.523
So, um, sometimes they get there and,
and there's very limited information.

00:13:18.893 --> 00:13:26.939
Um, uh, the, the process might not
be, um, uh, formalized, um, or there's

00:13:26.939 --> 00:13:32.235
an element to, uh, what we've seen
where, um, it's a, uh, somewhat of

00:13:32.235 --> 00:13:37.086
a, uh, an email us and, and we'll
do our best to resolve the issue.

00:13:37.391 --> 00:13:44.981
Um, and from what we've seen in our
survey, um, 72%, um, only offer,

00:13:45.016 --> 00:13:51.814
uh, an email or a form, uh, and a,
and a we'll try, um, but no owner,

00:13:51.835 --> 00:13:54.901
um, no timeline, and no procedure.

00:13:54.991 --> 00:13:59.374
So we're gonna talk about, um, some of the
ways that, uh, we'd recommend improving

00:13:59.397 --> 00:14:01.514
some of the policies that we've seen.

00:14:02.381 --> 00:14:09.007
Um, what we've seen on a lot of these
pages is, um, you know, a couple of,

00:14:09.085 --> 00:14:13.843
uh, variation of these two examples
and, um, these came from some, you

00:14:13.843 --> 00:14:18.063
know, large organizations that we
would expect, um, you know, some

00:14:18.074 --> 00:14:22.637
formalized accessibility policies,
and, uh, I'll read this wor- first one.

00:14:22.637 --> 00:14:24.358
It illustrates what we're talking about.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.865
"If because of a disability you're
experiencing difficulty accessing

00:14:27.866 --> 00:14:32.274
information on our website, please
contact us." Um, but that was very

00:14:32.274 --> 00:14:35.612
limited to what, um, uh, was offered.

00:14:35.694 --> 00:14:43.396
Uh, no formal process, uh, what, what we
would pos- uh, expect, a procedure, um,

00:14:43.418 --> 00:14:47.724
a level of, of formal, um, uh, guideline.

00:14:48.125 --> 00:14:52.910
Um, and then for this next one, they might
state, you know, for example, that they

00:14:52.913 --> 00:14:58.837
are, you know, trying to conform to a
certain level of D- WCAG, but it misses

00:14:58.838 --> 00:15:05.005
some of the, um, formal contact procedures
and what a user can expect when, um,

00:15:05.018 --> 00:15:08.241
getting in touch with these organizations.

00:15:09.414 --> 00:15:13.668
So talking about some organizations
that we've seen out there that have

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:20.286
really great accessibility policies,
um, a few here that we've named, uh,

00:15:20.286 --> 00:15:26.593
and reasons for each of these having
some really great, um, policies.

00:15:26.854 --> 00:15:30.507
Uh, the City of Aurora, you know,
they have a named coordinator.

00:15:30.655 --> 00:15:34.032
They state the level of
compliance, um, and they have a

00:15:34.034 --> 00:15:36.339
path for complaints and appeals.

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:39.769
Um, and then a few of these other
organizations, you know, they commit

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:44.478
to a response with a set number of
business days, so that gives a user,

00:15:44.598 --> 00:15:46.620
uh, what they can expect to receive.

00:15:47.003 --> 00:15:50.593
We hear a lot from, you know,
community members that they reach

00:15:50.594 --> 00:15:54.278
out to an organization, and they
never hear back, or they, they,

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:56.417
um, uh, don't know what to expect.

00:15:56.434 --> 00:16:00.524
You know, sending an im- you
know, a request out into, um, the

00:16:00.526 --> 00:16:04.943
void and, and not knowing what
to expect if, if anything at all.

00:16:05.409 --> 00:16:10.101
Um, couple of these other organizations,
they state a really firm, uh,

00:16:10.135 --> 00:16:14.799
turnaround time, uh, and that response
window for accessibility requests.

00:16:14.799 --> 00:16:19.596
So, you know, really give or, uh,
your customers, your, your, um,

00:16:19.609 --> 00:16:24.145
users a s- a, a sense of when they
can expect to hear back from you.

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:27.957
Um, this can be done at a,
a large organization and

00:16:27.958 --> 00:16:29.502
a very small organization.

00:16:29.663 --> 00:16:34.141
Setting up a process for
responding, um, is really important.

00:16:36.606 --> 00:16:37.236
Adam: Thanks, Adam.

00:16:38.021 --> 00:16:43.366
What we found in this survey is that
compliance language on paper does

00:16:43.367 --> 00:16:48.312
not translate to an accessible site
without a process to back it up.

00:16:48.728 --> 00:16:52.790
So you've heard us talk several times
already about is there a named owner,

00:16:53.037 --> 00:16:55.897
is there a person checking that inbox?

00:16:56.234 --> 00:16:58.917
Um, it is the core of all of this.

00:16:59.256 --> 00:17:03.951
So 27% of the accessibility
policies we surveyed claimed

00:17:03.951 --> 00:17:06.381
adherence to a WCAG level.

00:17:07.162 --> 00:17:11.377
What we found in testing these sites
is that for most of them, the claim

00:17:11.694 --> 00:17:13.279
was not backed up by the testing.

00:17:13.578 --> 00:17:14.816
And so why is that?

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:22.068
Simply put, citing WCAG 2.1 AA tells
you what someone believed on the day

00:17:22.070 --> 00:17:26.613
they published the site, but, you know,
it says nothing about whether the site

00:17:26.625 --> 00:17:31.478
is still being kept accessible, whether
there's that managed process behind it.

00:17:32.301 --> 00:17:36.967
Compliance language and actual
usability are two different things.

00:17:37.183 --> 00:17:40.954
Even inspections and actual
usability are two different things.

00:17:40.964 --> 00:17:44.278
So you really need that
human element to cover that.

00:17:46.937 --> 00:17:49.493
Next, let's talk about WordPress itself.

00:17:49.533 --> 00:17:52.716
This is why most of y'all are here today.

00:17:54.163 --> 00:17:59.298
And during our survey, what we found
is that a quarter of the pages that we

00:17:59.298 --> 00:18:02.628
surveyed, um, are using, uh, WordPress.

00:18:03.508 --> 00:18:09.293
Uh, this, we believe, is a little
low, um, lower than what I would

00:18:09.293 --> 00:18:11.048
have expect to see, one in four.

00:18:11.611 --> 00:18:15.817
Um, but as mentioned, our survey
methods might have undercounted

00:18:16.227 --> 00:18:20.192
the amount of WordPress sites
because we used the signatures on

00:18:20.194 --> 00:18:22.285
the page, and some pages hide that.

00:18:23.437 --> 00:18:26.377
Uh, what we found is that
most of these pages, a lot of

00:18:26.377 --> 00:18:28.166
these pages use page builders.

00:18:28.206 --> 00:18:33.854
Uh, the three most common ones we found
were Elementor, Divi, and WPBakery.

00:18:37.381 --> 00:18:41.495
So overlays covered-- overlays were
covered by some of the other talks,

00:18:41.498 --> 00:18:43.979
so I will not re-re-explain them here.

00:18:44.627 --> 00:18:50.636
But what I will say is that 9% of
the live sites in our study did run

00:18:50.653 --> 00:18:52.601
an accessibility overlay widget.

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:57.024
And here's the widgets that we
f- were most likely to find.

00:18:57.485 --> 00:19:05.386
Uh, UserWay with 3.6% of that
9%, accessiBe at 2.2, AudioEye

00:19:05.422 --> 00:19:11.841
at 1.9, Recite Me at 1.1, Equal
A- EqualWeb at half a percent.

00:19:12.557 --> 00:19:17.696
So this gives you an idea as
to the prevalence of automated

00:19:17.696 --> 00:19:19.217
solutions for accessibility.

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:27.367
We always warn our clients about the
overpromise of accessibility overlays.

00:19:28.111 --> 00:19:30.503
Uh, so we have a few data points here.

00:19:30.539 --> 00:19:33.450
One is from our own data,
from our own survey.

00:19:33.884 --> 00:19:38.404
That's that 38% of the sites
running an overlay also claim

00:19:38.435 --> 00:19:41.596
a WCAG level on that page.

00:19:42.267 --> 00:19:50.164
And as we know, the widgets are
not delivering full WCAG coverage.

00:19:50.609 --> 00:19:56.867
You can look to the DOJ and the FTC
for more information about that.

00:19:58.135 --> 00:20:03.066
Uh, two, from UsableNet's 2024
report, more than one in five

00:20:03.097 --> 00:20:07.310
digital accessibility lawsuits target
sites that already run a widget.

00:20:07.849 --> 00:20:11.476
So once more, overlays
don't stop lawsuits.

00:20:12.886 --> 00:20:18.771
Finally, in 2025, the FTC fined a
major overlay vendor $1 million for

00:20:18.782 --> 00:20:24.050
falsely claiming its widget can make
websites WCAG compliant on its own.

00:20:24.947 --> 00:20:29.826
So these sources are UsableNet.com,
ftc.gov, and you can check in

00:20:29.826 --> 00:20:31.280
on these when you get home.

00:20:32.795 --> 00:20:37.949
So how do we end up with
thousands of website accessibility

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:40.470
policy pages that are dead?

00:20:41.770 --> 00:20:46.031
Well… Or dead or, uh, boilerplate.

00:20:46.391 --> 00:20:51.297
So often we see that pages are
tool generated, they're using

00:20:51.311 --> 00:20:55.423
default templates, and they're
bundled in overlay software.

00:20:55.485 --> 00:21:00.642
So many overlay vendors include one-click
accessibility policy generators.

00:21:01.348 --> 00:21:08.789
Um, this leads to policies that are
one-click solutions, one and done.

00:21:08.891 --> 00:21:14.464
They're not checked, they're not,
um, they're not continuously updated.

00:21:14.575 --> 00:21:18.527
So every year you should be reviewing
your policy, and you should also be

00:21:18.527 --> 00:21:20.915
adapting to the latest standards.

00:21:24.511 --> 00:21:30.847
So let's talk about a policy
that actually opens the door to

00:21:30.848 --> 00:21:32.918
accessibility and what that looks like.

00:21:35.519 --> 00:21:35.979
Julian : Yeah.

00:21:36.004 --> 00:21:41.015
So, um, uh, illustrating some of
the pitfalls that we've seen across

00:21:41.019 --> 00:21:46.513
thousands of these accessibility pages
as well as, um, speaking with members

00:21:46.514 --> 00:21:50.313
of the community as to, you know, what
they would expect to see when, uh,

00:21:50.313 --> 00:21:54.986
they're trying to get in touch with an
organization or, or clicking, navigating

00:21:54.986 --> 00:21:58.897
to, um, an accessibility policy page.

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:02.832
Uh, and, um, that's really
accountability when it comes down to it.

00:22:02.868 --> 00:22:07.623
Um, you know, these five things that
we'll leave you with today, uh, a

00:22:07.625 --> 00:22:13.278
named owner, you know, somebody that is
accountable for the accessibility inbox.

00:22:13.594 --> 00:22:18.326
Um, you know, uh, I see this very
often that it's a generic inbox.

00:22:18.326 --> 00:22:23.888
It's the same inbox that routes
all of your, um, customer and

00:22:23.899 --> 00:22:29.988
organization's, um, uh, you know,
receipt of, of inbound communication,

00:22:29.988 --> 00:22:35.128
and often that is an opportunity
for, uh, these requests to get lost.

00:22:35.417 --> 00:22:44.054
Um, so a named owner and, uh, uh, m-
more so, more than one way for this

00:22:44.062 --> 00:22:49.370
person who might need to get in touch,
uh, with your organization to reach out.

00:22:49.416 --> 00:22:54.649
So if the form doesn't work, uh,
giving another opportunity in the

00:22:54.649 --> 00:23:00.695
case of, of, uh, failure, an email,
a phone number, um, and then a form.

00:23:00.815 --> 00:23:05.215
These are three ways of redundancy
that you can, um, you know,

00:23:05.233 --> 00:23:09.388
really offer something that's
usable for a person who needs it.

00:23:10.226 --> 00:23:16.463
Uh, we really like a stated response
time and a window of, uh, when

00:23:16.464 --> 00:23:20.555
they can expect or a person can
expect, uh, to hear back from you.

00:23:20.725 --> 00:23:24.994
State when they can hear back from you,
and make it a promise that you can keep.

00:23:25.244 --> 00:23:27.975
You know, an aggressive
response window is awesome.

00:23:27.975 --> 00:23:30.787
Make sure that you, um, can stick to it.

00:23:31.174 --> 00:23:36.947
Uh, you know, 24 hours, 48 hours, 72
hours, uh, these are pretty standard.

00:23:37.291 --> 00:23:42.664
Uh, that monitored inbox, we see a lot of
emails that go… You know, in addition

00:23:42.664 --> 00:23:49.722
to the pages going dead and dormant, we
test emails often to see if accessibility

00:23:49.722 --> 00:23:55.178
emails are active, monitored, uh,
and we get bounce backs a lot.

00:23:55.262 --> 00:24:00.774
Um, so when somebody reaches out to
one of your, um, you know, policies

00:24:00.774 --> 00:24:04.214
on, on that email, the same way
that they reach out to your privacy

00:24:04.214 --> 00:24:08.690
policy, this is another way, um, that
they're gonna get in touch with you.

00:24:08.694 --> 00:24:10.832
Make sure those emails, they work.

00:24:11.147 --> 00:24:15.530
Um, and then when you receive
one of these requests, make sure

00:24:15.532 --> 00:24:20.237
that you guys have planned, your
organization have, has planned for

00:24:20.269 --> 00:24:22.793
a complaint and escalation path.

00:24:23.071 --> 00:24:28.166
Um, so what happens when you
can't give this person the answer

00:24:28.167 --> 00:24:29.605
that they were looking for?

00:24:29.863 --> 00:24:34.852
Uh, what, what, what do you need to
do to take action on, on this request

00:24:34.853 --> 00:24:37.496
or this grievance, um, or complaint?

00:24:39.149 --> 00:24:40.774
Uh, Julian.

00:24:41.409 --> 00:24:46.212
Adam: So let's look at what it looks
like when we apply those concepts

00:24:46.233 --> 00:24:48.390
to a real accessibility page.

00:24:48.893 --> 00:24:53.273
We recently worked with a client to
improve their accessibility policy.

00:24:54.033 --> 00:25:01.303
Beforehand, they had six things, six
major things wrong with their policy page.

00:25:01.714 --> 00:25:02.858
There was no owner named.

00:25:03.594 --> 00:25:05.541
They were only providing an email.

00:25:06.391 --> 00:25:08.355
There was no response time provided.

00:25:09.020 --> 00:25:10.840
Their inbox was unmonitored.

00:25:11.391 --> 00:25:17.983
They had no documentation for how
escalation should work, and overall,

00:25:18.203 --> 00:25:23.461
they had an overlay doing most of their
work with, with regards to accessibility.

00:25:24.369 --> 00:25:29.129
Uh, that last one is more of a best
practice and less about the policy itself.

00:25:29.604 --> 00:25:32.864
So after, what does their
policy page look like?

00:25:32.864 --> 00:25:36.998
Well, they've gone ahead and named an
accessibility coordinator, something

00:25:36.999 --> 00:25:39.338
that they met internally to decide on.

00:25:40.059 --> 00:25:45.331
They added three forms of contact,
so not just an email, but a phone

00:25:45.339 --> 00:25:47.654
number and a form for redundancy.

00:25:48.206 --> 00:25:48.908
It's the whole point.

00:25:49.722 --> 00:25:54.562
Uh, they res- they added a deadline,
so they promised to respond within

00:25:54.563 --> 00:25:58.870
three minute- three business days,
and they met internally to figure

00:25:58.871 --> 00:26:00.463
out how to keep that promise.

00:26:01.601 --> 00:26:06.219
Their inbox is now being tested monthly
so that whenever an accessibility

00:26:06.219 --> 00:26:11.595
request or grievance comes in, it
will be responded to and, uh, the

00:26:11.606 --> 00:26:14.282
health of the inbox is checked.

00:26:15.130 --> 00:26:19.197
Uh, they had a clear complaint
and appeal process added.

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:24.580
So not only did they explain what it
was like to complain or to s- or to

00:26:24.582 --> 00:26:29.022
submit a request, but if the request
did not go through, how the user

00:26:29.024 --> 00:26:31.852
could appeal, um, for that request.

00:26:32.241 --> 00:26:35.830
So very, uh, mature
complaint process there.

00:26:36.688 --> 00:26:43.649
Uh, and finally, that best practice,
they're now using a real manual website

00:26:43.649 --> 00:26:48.637
inspector and a developer who understands
a little bit about accessibility.

00:26:48.964 --> 00:26:53.581
They're working together to fix the
barriers on the site rather than relying

00:26:53.966 --> 00:26:55.444
on an overlay for that sort of thing.

00:26:56.222 --> 00:27:01.828
So this week, we challenge you to do
three things, um, if you haven't already.

00:27:03.006 --> 00:27:09.607
First, assign an owner for accessibility,
someone who is going to regularly

00:27:09.617 --> 00:27:15.171
check the accessibility links, the
contact forms, and the request inbox.

00:27:15.769 --> 00:27:21.918
This person will likely also be checking
in on the inspection, uh, cycle.

00:27:21.934 --> 00:27:26.080
So how often are you inspecting
your website for barriers?

00:27:27.211 --> 00:27:29.677
Two, test your own intake process.

00:27:29.688 --> 00:27:33.954
So send an unprompted request
through your own contact channel

00:27:34.519 --> 00:27:37.206
and see if and when anyone answers.

00:27:37.699 --> 00:27:44.574
This, we found, is the most effective
way to not only test yourself, but

00:27:44.575 --> 00:27:51.648
also explain and illustrate to others
the importance of having that owner

00:27:51.648 --> 00:27:55.459
assigned and keeping accessibility
front and mi- front of mind.

00:27:56.903 --> 00:27:58.649
Three, commit to a time.

00:27:58.951 --> 00:28:02.175
So add one line to your policy.

00:28:02.878 --> 00:28:07.160
Say, for example, we respond
within five business days.

00:28:07.163 --> 00:28:08.721
You could use three business days.

00:28:08.723 --> 00:28:10.137
You could use seven business days.

00:28:10.449 --> 00:28:15.238
Find a time that you will
actually be able to commit to.

00:28:15.824 --> 00:28:19.305
'Cause what's even worse is
promising a short turnaround time

00:28:19.307 --> 00:28:23.172
and not meeting that, um, that
time that you've set yourself.

00:28:23.533 --> 00:28:24.793
So keep that promise.

00:28:25.094 --> 00:28:31.262
Meet internally, have these conversations,
and figure out amongst yourself who's

00:28:31.264 --> 00:28:35.272
gonna be that owner, who is gonna
be checking on these accessibility

00:28:35.273 --> 00:28:41.311
features, and who is going to be
responding to those requests, and how

00:28:41.312 --> 00:28:42.934
much time should they have to do that?

00:28:46.559 --> 00:28:47.698
Julian : Yeah, thanks, Julian.

00:28:48.320 --> 00:28:51.918
Uh, I, um, I, I couldn't agree more.

00:28:51.961 --> 00:28:56.360
Um, what we've seen across so many
of these accessibility policies,

00:28:56.683 --> 00:29:00.375
uh, is, um, folks are reaching
out and they don't hear back.

00:29:00.693 --> 00:29:02.821
They receive bounce-back emails.

00:29:02.942 --> 00:29:07.947
Um, the organizations that don't have
an accessibility policy at all, this is

00:29:07.947 --> 00:29:10.559
a really good opportunity to adopt one.

00:29:10.983 --> 00:29:15.726
As Julian said, have those conversations
internally and start to figure out

00:29:15.726 --> 00:29:17.550
what makes sense for your organization.

00:29:17.916 --> 00:29:22.170
Uh, a process and how to keep
it, um, is really important.

00:29:22.576 --> 00:29:27.668
Um, what we've seen across so many of
the accessibility policies that our team

00:29:27.677 --> 00:29:34.991
has inspected, uh, 23% of the policy
pages across thousands of the pages we've

00:29:35.184 --> 00:29:38.353
reviewed are dead, uh, are inactive.

00:29:38.600 --> 00:29:44.141
Um, so if you take away anything
from today, uh, go to your page right

00:29:44.143 --> 00:29:49.370
away, scroll to the bottom, often
where your accessibility policies

00:29:49.624 --> 00:29:51.385
are living is in that footer.

00:29:51.838 --> 00:29:53.154
Click the page.

00:29:53.351 --> 00:29:54.519
Does it load?

00:29:55.391 --> 00:29:56.813
Are you getting an error?

00:29:57.090 --> 00:30:01.260
Um, that is something that we're
seeing across so many of these

00:30:01.265 --> 00:30:05.161
pages, that they've, um, just
gone dormant and, and, and dead.

00:30:05.585 --> 00:30:11.226
Um- Once a person makes it to that
policy page, so if you're testing your

00:30:11.226 --> 00:30:14.057
own page, click that link, read it.

00:30:14.707 --> 00:30:16.413
Do they have a process?

00:30:16.529 --> 00:30:17.835
Do they have an email?

00:30:17.966 --> 00:30:21.946
Is there, um, somebody that
they can get in touch with?

00:30:22.213 --> 00:30:26.275
Uh, so many of these accessibility
policies have a date.

00:30:26.630 --> 00:30:30.177
Uh, many of them are years old.

00:30:30.427 --> 00:30:34.499
Uh, that's a really clear way for me
when I, when I notice that a- a- an

00:30:34.501 --> 00:30:39.958
accessibility policy hasn't been updated
for many years, um, it's probably time

00:30:39.958 --> 00:30:42.702
to consider or reconsider accessibility.

00:30:43.084 --> 00:30:50.495
Um, and of many of the pages that
we're reviewing, clients are asking

00:30:50.496 --> 00:30:57.791
us about overlays, uh, automation,
uh, remediation tools to make the,

00:30:57.805 --> 00:31:01.912
the job easier, um, trust but verify.

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:09.697
So many of these, um, uh, plug-ins and
tools might auto-inject an accessibility

00:31:09.705 --> 00:31:11.631
policy that you have not read.

00:31:11.942 --> 00:31:15.377
Uh, many of these plug-ins,
they have that option.

00:31:15.635 --> 00:31:22.056
Um, so make sure that if you're using one
of these plug-ins, click through that,

00:31:22.082 --> 00:31:27.612
that functionality and read the policy
that that plug-in might be injecting.

00:31:27.911 --> 00:31:31.532
Is it in conflict with the,
um, policy that you might

00:31:31.563 --> 00:31:33.935
already have on your, your page?

00:31:34.236 --> 00:31:35.544
Um, is it accurate?

00:31:35.825 --> 00:31:39.670
Um, so, you know, that's something
that we often notice as well.

00:31:40.877 --> 00:31:45.807
Anyway, uh, I think many of the
takeaways will be similar across

00:31:45.807 --> 00:31:47.625
so many of the talks that you hear.

00:31:47.911 --> 00:31:54.546
Uh, you know, accessibility is not just
about a, a, a one-and-done exercise.

00:31:54.763 --> 00:31:56.228
This is a process.

00:31:56.404 --> 00:32:02.680
So as you develop your process, uh,
make sure that you're thinking and,

00:32:02.687 --> 00:32:07.467
and not forgetting about, uh, your
accessibility policy, that public front,

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:10.481
that public-facing, uh, uh, statement.

00:32:10.863 --> 00:32:14.926
Um, you know, don't just
publish your policy, but build a

00:32:14.926 --> 00:32:17.707
process around how you keep it.

00:32:18.630 --> 00:32:20.105
So thanks for having us.

00:32:20.150 --> 00:32:25.378
Um, really, uh, uh, gl- happy, uh, to
be here, and thanks for the opportunity.

00:32:25.422 --> 00:32:31.495
Um, happy to take questions about,
um, how we came up with the analysis,

00:32:31.498 --> 00:32:36.466
what our survey looked like, uh, and
any, um, you know, recommendations

00:32:36.466 --> 00:32:40.713
or questions you might have about
your own, uh, accessibility policies

00:32:40.713 --> 00:32:43.308
and processes, uh, internally.

00:32:43.338 --> 00:32:45.372
But, um, happy to take any Q&A.

00:32:51.210 --> 00:32:52.354
Kadian: Fantastic.

00:32:52.375 --> 00:32:53.854
That was a really great talk.

00:32:54.636 --> 00:32:58.484
Um, I r- w- we have some great
questions, so we're gonna dive right in.

00:32:58.486 --> 00:32:59.731
I've kinda triaged this.

00:33:00.086 --> 00:33:01.924
I wanna start with Jessica's question.

00:33:01.966 --> 00:33:04.930
Um, please explain the difference
between the accessibility statement

00:33:05.221 --> 00:33:07.038
and an accessibility policy.

00:33:07.276 --> 00:33:10.007
I've published a statement page,
but our company doesn't have a,

00:33:10.361 --> 00:33:12.726
have a policy-level documentation.

00:33:12.999 --> 00:33:14.937
I think that's a great
question to start out with.

00:33:17.733 --> 00:33:17.988
Adam: Yeah.

00:33:17.994 --> 00:33:24.326
So, uh, the main difference is that a
statement is, uh, more public-facing, so

00:33:24.326 --> 00:33:30.493
it's going to tell your users that, um,
this is how we approach accessibility.

00:33:31.208 --> 00:33:37.962
Uh, a policy is internal, so it is
designed to, uh, inform the s- the

00:33:37.976 --> 00:33:43.287
people working in the organization how
to actually deliver on that promise

00:33:43.580 --> 00:33:44.896
that is laid out in the statement.

00:33:45.464 --> 00:33:48.569
So policy oftentimes is more complex.

00:33:48.569 --> 00:33:50.908
It's more, uh, it's bigger.

00:33:50.930 --> 00:33:54.800
It involves all the different
roles in your organization that

00:33:54.817 --> 00:33:56.675
are related to accessibility.

00:33:58.255 --> 00:33:59.933
I hope that helps explain.

00:34:00.709 --> 00:34:01.098
Kadian: Yeah.

00:34:01.642 --> 00:34:07.117
Um, and coming in from, from the,
you know, breaking it down, um,

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:11.846
can you partially conform to a WCAG
level with named exceptions, or

00:34:11.848 --> 00:34:13.369
say what you've tested with, i.e.

00:34:13.373 --> 00:34:17.406
screen reader, keyboard navigation
on main pages, but nested pages with

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:19.231
low traffic might not be compliant?

00:34:20.829 --> 00:34:21.929
Julian : Julian, you
wanna take that one too?

00:34:21.929 --> 00:34:23.219
I think the answer is yes.

00:34:23.912 --> 00:34:25.554
Adam: Yeah, uh, definitely.

00:34:26.043 --> 00:34:29.405
Um, it is actually, uh, the
honest way to do it, right?

00:34:29.455 --> 00:34:30.010
So-

00:34:30.116 --> 00:34:30.304
Kadian: Mm-hmm

00:34:30.304 --> 00:34:34.509
…  Adam: um, your conformance,
uh, is done page by page.

00:34:34.527 --> 00:34:37.378
Accessibility needs to
happen case by case.

00:34:37.766 --> 00:34:41.493
Um, and so you can lay out
which pages have been tested

00:34:41.672 --> 00:34:43.367
or how they've been tested.

00:34:43.391 --> 00:34:48.016
So you can, uh, explain that you're
testing with screen readers, keyboards.

00:34:48.377 --> 00:34:55.202
Um, and also, uh, in some cases,
listing known gaps actually shows that

00:34:55.202 --> 00:34:58.775
you are committed to accessibility
and transparent about the process.

00:34:59.219 --> 00:35:05.251
Um, there's some, uh, hesitation with
that because, uh, organizations are

00:35:05.251 --> 00:35:09.387
afraid of opening them up, opening
themselves up to more exposure.

00:35:09.708 --> 00:35:14.167
But if you do it in a smart way,
um, it actually can add to your,

00:35:14.290 --> 00:35:17.764
uh, to your presence and your
approach with accessibility.

00:35:18.167 --> 00:35:19.573
I don't know if I missed
anything there, Adam.

00:35:20.332 --> 00:35:20.743
Julian : No, no.

00:35:20.807 --> 00:35:21.109
Perfect.

00:35:21.517 --> 00:35:21.848
Adam: Thanks.

00:35:23.081 --> 00:35:23.451
Kadian: All right.

00:35:23.483 --> 00:35:29.587
And, um, combining a couple questions
here that are both on the, um, the,

00:35:29.588 --> 00:35:33.000
uh, um, training and employee thing.

00:35:33.154 --> 00:35:37.695
Uh, n- excuse me, not thing, uh,
just, uh, ground level there.

00:35:38.238 --> 00:35:42.996
Um, I'm gonna read out Amber's
full question because I think

00:35:42.996 --> 00:35:46.279
it also helps answer a lot of
the other questions going on.

00:35:46.729 --> 00:35:51.267
Um, we had someone complain once
that they couldn't contact us for

00:35:51.267 --> 00:35:53.203
accessibility support via text.

00:35:53.602 --> 00:35:54.388
They were deaf.

00:35:54.547 --> 00:35:59.033
We provide support via email and phone,
but they felt it wasn't sufficient,

00:35:59.465 --> 00:36:01.774
and, uh, expected to be able to text.

00:36:01.988 --> 00:36:05.973
What opinions do you have on the best
methods to offer, especially for small

00:36:05.974 --> 00:36:08.818
teams that can't monitor many channels?

00:36:12.881 --> 00:36:16.553
Julian : So the, uh, the reality is
that there's nothing out there that I

00:36:16.554 --> 00:36:20.763
can say and point to that says that,
you know, you as an organization will

00:36:20.795 --> 00:36:24.115
require text as a communication channel.

00:36:24.438 --> 00:36:30.493
Um, my opinion, and I've set this up
for a number of clients, and I've seen

00:36:30.493 --> 00:36:35.745
a number of clients do it successfully,
is text is a very accessible channel now

00:36:35.963 --> 00:36:42.549
for both, uh, your community externally
as well as you internally to set up, uh,

00:36:42.571 --> 00:36:47.588
a line so that you can, um, have text
as a communication channel and an, an

00:36:47.588 --> 00:36:50.120
inbound channel for your organization.

00:36:50.406 --> 00:36:53.232
Um, I think it's a great
communication channel.

00:36:53.367 --> 00:36:55.296
It is indeed accessible.

00:36:55.480 --> 00:37:01.258
The way that people use their
mobile device is already set up,

00:37:01.259 --> 00:37:02.872
and it's so personal to them.

00:37:02.877 --> 00:37:08.363
If you have one of these lived
experiences, y- you know, low

00:37:08.365 --> 00:37:13.102
vision, total blindness, mobile,
you know, a number of, of abilities,

00:37:13.119 --> 00:37:18.441
range of abilities, um, using that
mobile device, um, and engaging

00:37:18.443 --> 00:37:20.692
in a text is, is, is pretty easy.

00:37:21.046 --> 00:37:27.026
Um, you know, I, I would say that
having multiple channels is a

00:37:27.026 --> 00:37:28.908
really great idea, not having one.

00:37:29.437 --> 00:37:32.461
Um, so don't just rely on
one communication channel.

00:37:32.462 --> 00:37:34.780
In the case of failure, have a redundancy.

00:37:35.074 --> 00:37:39.755
And so having an email and having
a phone number is typically, um,

00:37:39.765 --> 00:37:41.670
the most common and the easiest.

00:37:41.933 --> 00:37:44.618
Um, but I'd push you guys to do text.

00:37:44.678 --> 00:37:45.392
I think it's a great idea.

00:37:49.371 --> 00:37:54.427
Kadian: Um, along, uh, the, again,
going in, back into, uh, internal

00:37:54.438 --> 00:37:58.114
company, uh, what resources do you
recommend for training employees

00:37:58.127 --> 00:38:00.091
on support accessibility policies?

00:38:00.782 --> 00:38:06.790
Julian : Yeah, not, um, I, not
everyone can do the IA- IAAP,

00:38:07.047 --> 00:38:12.510
um, uh, uh, curriculum to get
some of the certifications, but,

00:38:12.819 --> 00:38:17.479
um, that, um, curriculum that
they do offer anybody can take.

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:23.279
You know, the, the courses and the,
the descriptions and the, um, publicly

00:38:23.279 --> 00:38:30.139
available, um, materials that are
out there are so readily available.

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:35.261
Uh, YouTube, um, I, I would
really encourage, um, you to look

00:38:35.265 --> 00:38:38.719
up some of the, um, curriculum.

00:38:39.012 --> 00:38:43.145
Um, it's actually, uh, interesting
how IAAP does it, where they

00:38:43.146 --> 00:38:48.627
encourage their community as part
of my continuing education, one

00:38:48.635 --> 00:38:53.805
thing that I can do is develop and,
and teach curriculum to others.

00:38:53.966 --> 00:38:55.927
And so there's a lot of that out there.

00:38:55.934 --> 00:39:00.182
Some is definitely better than others,
but the International Accessibility

00:39:00.184 --> 00:39:07.134
Association Professionals International,
um, uh, uh, uh, group, um, has a

00:39:07.136 --> 00:39:09.166
list of some of these materials.

00:39:09.367 --> 00:39:12.971
There's like 100 links, and I
encourage you to, um, check those out.

00:39:13.769 --> 00:39:17.857
Adam: Uh, section508.gov
also has some good resources.

00:39:18.121 --> 00:39:23.555
Um, but as Adam mentioned, I love a good
video, um, and some visual explanation.

00:39:26.489 --> 00:39:29.649
Kadian: Um, Adam, these are probably
both gonna be for you because

00:39:29.649 --> 00:39:30.536
they've already been answered.

00:39:30.890 --> 00:39:33.420
Uh, but they're really great, so I
wanna make sure everybody sees them.

00:39:33.464 --> 00:39:37.180
Um, and I'm gonna start with, uh, could
you give your opinion or guidance on using

00:39:37.180 --> 00:39:39.008
the accessibility statement generator?

00:39:39.477 --> 00:39:43.720
Uh, it's not enough, but can it
be a short-term stopgap solution?

00:39:46.946 --> 00:39:51.165
Julian : I, I have to be careful with
how I, um, say this because, um, my

00:39:51.165 --> 00:39:59.218
opinion is, uh, uh, you should go out
and look at your site right now and

00:39:59.229 --> 00:40:05.161
see if, one, you have one of these
plugins already enabled in the bottom

00:40:05.161 --> 00:40:07.145
right corner, bottom left, whatever.

00:40:07.428 --> 00:40:12.769
Um, click it and see if that
plugin is already inserting a, a

00:40:12.790 --> 00:40:15.937
statement or a policy onto your page.

00:40:16.161 --> 00:40:17.629
Um, have you read it?

00:40:17.969 --> 00:40:19.325
Has your legal team read it?

00:40:19.433 --> 00:40:23.270
Are you prepared to, um,
uh, meet, meet the standard?

00:40:23.633 --> 00:40:31.132
Um, uh, I've noticed one of the larger,
um, accessibility plugin vendors has

00:40:31.132 --> 00:40:36.507
a spelling error in the, um, uh, and a
grammatical error in w- in, in some of the

00:40:36.511 --> 00:40:39.616
statements that they've inserted on all.

00:40:39.663 --> 00:40:43.333
I, I can't… I don't know for certain,
but, um, it's missing a period.

00:40:43.538 --> 00:40:47.799
Um, so I, I know that there's a number
of business owners and organizations

00:40:47.799 --> 00:40:49.313
that are, are not reading it.

00:40:49.337 --> 00:40:54.125
It is, um, a stop gap, but
nothing more, in my opinion.

00:40:54.125 --> 00:40:56.343
You should be developing your own policy.

00:40:56.596 --> 00:40:58.656
You should be reading your policy.

00:40:58.982 --> 00:41:01.511
Um, to answer the question, Julian?

00:41:01.953 --> 00:41:02.259
Adam: Yeah.

00:41:02.424 --> 00:41:07.381
Part of developing that, that policy
internally will be producing a statement.

00:41:07.702 --> 00:41:12.390
And, um, you know, you don't wanna wait
until that's done to put your statement

00:41:12.392 --> 00:41:16.261
online, but that's the process that
you'll use to improve and build on.

00:41:17.985 --> 00:41:18.173
And-

00:41:18.173 --> 00:41:18.781
Kadian: So ultimate-

00:41:19.489 --> 00:41:19.913
Adam: Oh, I'm sorry.

00:41:19.933 --> 00:41:25.618
Just one thing that's missed a lot is that
on an accessibility statement page, you

00:41:25.618 --> 00:41:29.365
know, there's the boilerplate stuff and
everything we talked about today, which

00:41:29.366 --> 00:41:34.680
is, you know, enhancing it with contact
information, uh, clearly outlining steps.

00:41:35.111 --> 00:41:40.406
But, you know, keep in mind, Adam
began this talk s- reminding folks

00:41:40.409 --> 00:41:45.882
that, um, a lot of people that
require assistive technology and rely

00:41:45.884 --> 00:41:48.509
on these, they are planning ahead.

00:41:48.744 --> 00:41:53.621
They are going to an organization's
website ahead of time to try to get

00:41:53.634 --> 00:41:59.000
information about, um, everything from
digital to physical accessibility.

00:41:59.476 --> 00:42:05.652
Um, so that is also a great place to
be featuring that information, make

00:42:05.652 --> 00:42:10.571
it super easy to access, and make that
page accessible as well, of course.

00:42:11.949 --> 00:42:12.188
Totally.

00:42:13.305 --> 00:42:19.313
Kadian: So, uh, uh, to put it w- w-
s- any stop gap, you just have to

00:42:19.316 --> 00:42:20.950
be prepared for the consequences.

00:42:21.323 --> 00:42:23.932
So is, is that, is that
a way to think about it?

00:42:23.948 --> 00:42:29.296
Is anything that you think of as a stop
gap or good enough for that time, it's,

00:42:29.386 --> 00:42:33.235
uh, only good as far as you're willing
to put up with the consequences for it?

00:42:33.710 --> 00:42:34.020
Julian : Yeah.

00:42:34.020 --> 00:42:39.978
I mean, I would say that if you re- if
you, um, if you today go and on your site

00:42:39.978 --> 00:42:44.942
right now and you pop open your plugin and
you actually read that statement, which

00:42:44.942 --> 00:42:49.298
it might be the first time that many of
you have, and I, I hear it all the time.

00:42:49.542 --> 00:42:51.399
And it's not to be
something embarrassed about.

00:42:51.399 --> 00:42:52.928
Let's just get it fixed right away.

00:42:53.167 --> 00:42:56.819
But if you go read that statement
and you're happy with it, then great.

00:42:57.295 --> 00:43:02.757
Um, you know, if your attorney, if your
insurance provider, if your website

00:43:02.761 --> 00:43:06.803
developer, you know, if everybody on,
in your organization is comfortable with

00:43:06.803 --> 00:43:11.937
the language that you've put on that
site as is, no changes, then go for it.

00:43:11.937 --> 00:43:13.134
But highly doubt it.

00:43:13.810 --> 00:43:15.307
Adam: Need, needs human eyes on it.

00:43:15.676 --> 00:43:15.940
Julian : Mm-hmm.

00:43:15.940 --> 00:43:16.874
Adam: Human review, right.

00:43:16.984 --> 00:43:20.357
Julian : And we've heard that in a
lot of these conversations is, is,

00:43:20.384 --> 00:43:26.881
is automation requires, um, you know,
some tolerance for the, the tool.

00:43:26.903 --> 00:43:28.853
There's some great tools
out there, you know?

00:43:29.368 --> 00:43:34.319
I- AI is great in, in a lot of cases,
but, you know, are you reading it?

00:43:34.399 --> 00:43:36.763
You know, are you, you know,
fa- you know, trust but verify.

00:43:37.442 --> 00:43:39.571
Kadian: It's for humans, not for AI.

00:43:40.761 --> 00:43:43.134
Adam: Adam's mentioning one
more trend that we're seeing.

00:43:43.339 --> 00:43:49.156
These accessibility plugins are
now often, um, offering, uh, a

00:43:49.259 --> 00:43:51.895
accessibility statement, uh, generator.

00:43:52.307 --> 00:43:56.230
Um, not all of them are doing
it, but we've seen it more often.

00:43:56.558 --> 00:44:01.609
And I would, uh, you know, same,
same guidance here from Adam.

00:44:01.623 --> 00:44:04.535
Make sure that it's saying what you
want, and if not, just make your

00:44:04.535 --> 00:44:06.296
own accessibility statement page.

00:44:06.310 --> 00:44:07.739
It's usually a better quality.

00:44:10.240 --> 00:44:13.185
Kadian: Uh, and going right into
the, you know, we have to be,

00:44:13.186 --> 00:44:16.210
uh, a little bit tricky here is,
uh, this is another question that

00:44:16.229 --> 00:44:18.063
Adam answered in the, uh, Q&A.

00:44:18.608 --> 00:44:23.153
Uh, we had the Recite Me and took it down
because we were concerned it would flag

00:44:23.153 --> 00:44:25.193
us, uh, for people just looking to sue.

00:44:25.333 --> 00:44:25.977
Opinion

00:44:28.794 --> 00:44:29.634
-  Julian : not an attorney.

00:44:29.698 --> 00:44:34.640
Uh, but I have worked on a number
of these cases, and I think that the

00:44:34.648 --> 00:44:38.226
pendulum swit- swings one way or another.

00:44:38.474 --> 00:44:43.476
Um, Julian just led an amazing
conversation with one of our

00:44:43.477 --> 00:44:48.051
government entities talking about,
um, overlays and how they fit and

00:44:48.051 --> 00:44:49.486
how they should be relied upon.

00:44:49.488 --> 00:44:55.010
But the DOJ itself has said very
clearly, if you go to their website, that

00:44:55.025 --> 00:44:57.265
automation should not be relied upon.

00:44:57.465 --> 00:45:00.511
And that is the guidance that we
take and the guidance that I'll

00:45:00.511 --> 00:45:05.386
say on this call because that's,
that's what's out there for us here.

00:45:05.679 --> 00:45:12.154
Um, so, um, I, you know, we've tested…
Every test that we do and inspection that

00:45:12.155 --> 00:45:18.986
we perform always includes a step where
we test if those tools are working to

00:45:18.987 --> 00:45:21.692
resolve the barriers that we identified.

00:45:21.954 --> 00:45:25.799
And, um, not all of them are, are solved.

00:45:25.847 --> 00:45:29.384
Every day it maybe gets better
and better, but, um, I would say

00:45:29.411 --> 00:45:34.974
that, uh, the pendulum has shifted
back and forth depending on, um,

00:45:35.041 --> 00:45:36.542
the area of the country you're in.

00:45:37.096 --> 00:45:43.401
Uh, the, um, uh, you know, some of
the attorneys that I have spoken with

00:45:43.481 --> 00:45:47.779
feel that way, that these plugins,
depending on the sophistication

00:45:47.791 --> 00:45:55.974
of the, um, uh, of the, the case,
uh, uh, it, it could cause, um…

00:45:56.142 --> 00:45:57.657
You know, it could attract.

00:45:57.947 --> 00:46:00.398
Um, I- it's hard for me to say.

00:46:00.398 --> 00:46:03.179
I don't, I don't really have the
data, but, um, that's my opinion.

00:46:04.032 --> 00:46:05.782
Adam: I would, I would just add to that.

00:46:06.405 --> 00:46:12.590
Automated tools like plugins, they
have a place in the testing, um,

00:46:12.705 --> 00:46:17.919
process, but what happens is that
most teams start and end there.

00:46:18.531 --> 00:46:24.248
Um, whereas the automation needs to be
kind of like at the top of the funnel,

00:46:24.742 --> 00:46:27.745
catching the easy-to-catch barriers.

00:46:28.045 --> 00:46:28.164
Sherose: Mm-hmm.

00:46:28.213 --> 00:46:31.663
Adam: And then next you're doing,
what we really need to do is be

00:46:31.728 --> 00:46:36.601
hiring an inspector to do testing
with assistive technology, 'cause

00:46:36.631 --> 00:46:40.907
that is the technology that your
actual, uh, website users are using.

00:46:41.414 --> 00:46:44.433
And if you're not testing with
those, you're not gonna catch

00:46:44.435 --> 00:46:48.701
the barriers, and that's what,
uh, a lot of these plugins do.

00:46:51.641 --> 00:46:52.226
Kadian: Great answer.

00:46:52.961 --> 00:46:56.121
Uh, we have, um, just… We have
about three more minutes left.

00:46:56.219 --> 00:46:57.941
We have this last question here.

00:46:58.426 --> 00:47:01.275
Uh, and this actually goes
ba- right back to your study.

00:47:01.799 --> 00:47:05.485
Did you track disproportionate
burden across policies/statements?

00:47:06.263 --> 00:47:10.900
Adam: You know, we did not, but that
is something that we will probably

00:47:10.901 --> 00:47:13.079
be tracking, um, moving forward.

00:47:13.252 --> 00:47:17.103
It's, it's hard to, it's hard
to make that determination.

00:47:17.615 --> 00:47:25.305
Um, and I don't know of many
statements that include, uh, you

00:47:25.305 --> 00:47:27.325
know, wording around, um, that.

00:47:28.120 --> 00:47:32.464
Um, I think that hopefully
answers your question.

00:47:33.231 --> 00:47:33.649
Julian : I'd agree.

00:47:34.411 --> 00:47:34.704
Kadian: Yeah.

00:47:35.596 --> 00:47:35.934
All right.

00:47:35.942 --> 00:47:40.627
Do you guys have any last, m- like,
what's your, what's your ta- if you

00:47:40.628 --> 00:47:43.942
have… If anybody can take away one
thing from this, what would you recommend?

00:47:44.378 --> 00:47:44.602
Julian : Sure.

00:47:44.603 --> 00:47:49.696
I would say just go home and read
your… Go to your employer if you work

00:47:49.698 --> 00:47:54.091
for somebody, if you manage a site, if
you have a policy, if you use a plugin.

00:47:54.349 --> 00:47:59.157
Go see if you already have an
accessibility statement, read it, and

00:47:59.159 --> 00:48:01.560
confirm that, um, you know, you like it.

00:48:01.819 --> 00:48:04.002
If you don't, it's time
to make some improvements.

00:48:04.220 --> 00:48:07.436
And I would also, you know,
remind everyone that your

00:48:07.436 --> 00:48:12.548
accessibility policy serves so
much more than just the checkbox.

00:48:12.550 --> 00:48:17.012
I, I, I think someone somewhere
told someone s- told us that to have

00:48:17.012 --> 00:48:18.985
this page is a really good checkbox.

00:48:19.223 --> 00:48:24.289
It's a, a really good reminder
today that these pages serve a

00:48:24.293 --> 00:48:26.482
community of people who need it.

00:48:26.792 --> 00:48:31.666
So if you're an organization e-commerce
or if you operate a brick-and-mortar,

00:48:31.961 --> 00:48:37.512
put information on the page that, um,
talks to the accommodations you offer.

00:48:37.999 --> 00:48:38.501
Um…

00:48:40.834 --> 00:48:44.325
Adam: And please, uh, make
sure to leave us feedback.

